      "text": "To determine the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, we need to find all pairs of positive integers \\((n, m)\\) such that \\( n \\cdot m = 86,400 \\). This is equivalent to finding all the factorizations of 86,400 into two positive factors.\n\nFirst, we perform the prime factorization of 86,400:\n\\[\n86,400 = 864 \\times 100 = (2^5 \\times 3^3 \\times 10) \\times (2^2 \\times 5^2) = 2^{7} \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\nUsing the prime factorization, we can find all the factors of 86,400. Specifically, we need to count the number of ways to partition 86,400 into two positive factors. This is given by the number of divisors of 86,400.\n\nThe number of divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \\cdots p_k^{e_k} \\) is calculated as:\n\\[\n(e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n\\]\nFor \\( 86,400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2 \\), the number of divisors is:\n\\[\n(7 + 1)(3 + 1)(2 + 1) = 8 \\times 4 \\times 3 = 96\n\\]\nTherefore, there are 96 ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, where \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{96}\n\\]",
