      "text": "To determine how many ways a day can be divided into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, we need to find all pairs of positive integers \\((n, m)\\) such that the product \\( n \\times m = 86400 \\).\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorization of 86400:\n\\[\n86400 \\div 2 = 43200\n\\]\n\\[\n43200 \\div 2 = 21600\n\\]\n\\[\n21600 \\div 2 = 10800\n\\]\n\\[\n10800 \\div 2 = 5400\n\\]\n\\[\n5400 \\div 2 = 2700\n\\]\n\\[\n2700 \\div 2 = 1350\n\\]\n\\[\n1350 \\div 2 = 675\n\\]\n\\[\n675 \\div 3 = 225\n\\]\n\\[\n225 \\div 3 = 75\n\\]\n\\[\n75 \\div 3 = 25\n\\]\n\\[\n25 \\div 5 = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\div 5 = 1\n\\]\nThus, the prime factorization of 86400 is:\n\\[\n86400 = 2^7 \\times 3^3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find all the pairs \\((n, m)\\) such that \\( n \\times m = 86400 \\). Each pair corresponds to a way to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds. We need to count all the divisors of 86400.\n\nThe total number of divisors of 86400 can be found by adding 1 to each of the exponents in the prime factorization and then multiplying the results:\n\\[\n(7+1)(3+1)(2+1) = 8 \\times 4 \\times 3 = 96\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, where \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers, is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{96}\n\\]",
